# ## 🛠️ **Solutions to Fix SSM Access**

# ### **Option 1: Update Lambda IAM Role (Recommended)**

# Add these permissions to the `oncall-iam` role:

# ```json
# {
#   "Version": "2012-10-17",
#   "Statement": [
#     {
#       "Effect": "Allow",
#       "Action": [
#         "ssm:SendCommand",
#         "ssm:GetCommandInvocation",
#         "ssm:DescribeInstanceInformation",
#         "ssm:ListCommands",
#         "ssm:ListCommandInvocations"
#       ],
#       "Resource": [
#         "arn:aws:ec2:*:*:instance/*",
#         "arn:aws:ssm:*:*:document/AWS-RunShellScript",
#         "arn:aws:ssm:*:*:*"
#       ]
#     }
#   ]
# }
# ```

# ### **Option 2: Use AWS CLI Directly**

# ```bash
# # Add SSM permissions to your AWS CLI profile, then run:
# aws ssm send-command \
#     --instance-ids "i-03326536e3f1be717" \
#     --document-name "AWS-RunShellScript" \
#     --parameters 'commands=["ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -20"]' \
#     --region us-east-1
# ```

# ### **Option 3: SSH Direct Access**

# ```bash
# # If you have the oncall-ec2-key:
# ssh -i /path/to/oncall-ec2-key.pem ec2-user@18.207.205.56
# # Then run: ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -20
# ```

